<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Alupa dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">200 CE – 1444 CE (approx.) · capital Mangalore</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The long-lived rulers of Tulu Nadu on the Karnataka coast, who survived for more than a thousand years as vassals of greater powers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Alupas ruled the coastal strip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>. Ptolemy may refer to their land as Olokhoira in the 2nd century, and they appear in inscriptions from the 7th century as feudatories of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami</a>. Earlier they probably served the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kadamba-dynasty">Kadamba dynasty</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their capitals moved from Udyavara to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangalore">Mangalore</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barkur">Barkur</a>. Alupa kings issued distinctive gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins and ruled through local chiefs, profiting from the trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanara">Kanara</a> ports with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. They acknowledged the supremacy in turn of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire</a>, and patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaiva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jain</a> temples. Their inscriptions are among the earliest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 14th century the Alupas came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, and their line ends in the records around 1444, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulu-nadu">Tulu Nadu</a> was absorbed into Vijayanagara administration. The region kept its distinct Tulu language and culture.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/alupa-dynasty.jpg" alt="Alupa dynasty" width="320" height="166"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alupas._Uncertain_ruler._14th_century._Chattopadhyaya_Type_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 CE – 1444 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mangalore</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aluvarasa I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kannada, Tulu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Alupas, Aluvas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=822&amp;focus=alupa-dynasty" aria-label="Alupa dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6802 1340 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Alupa dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7097 1795h0M7084 1941h0M7088 1864h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7122 1596h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7122 1596h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day India. <a href="/atlas?year=822&amp;focus=alupa-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

